[Music]  hello and welcome to the session on  DevOps environment in various  organizations that adopt DevOps as a  software development and delivery  methodology we will look at how  organizations should set up a DevOps  environment in terms of technologies  tools and also for their teams  culturally what changes they should  bring within the organization to have a  successful DevOps transition and what  are the various challenges that  organizations can run into when bringing  about these DevOps environment changes  for an organization DevOps adoption not  only means a lot of tools and software  for the team to learn and understand of  course that is there it also does not  mean you begin to do your deployments in  a cloud environment that is just one  part of adopting DevOps DevOps option  not only means that you do your  deployments in a docker containers  certainly that is one way of doing your  development and deployment and DevOps  and adoption not only means that your  dev and operations now collaborate  better of course all of that is required  for you to be technology upgraded and  tools upgraded however apart from this  there are a lot of cultural changes  within the organization within the  entire organization and not just the  development teams and your operation  teams to understand this better it is  important to learn that DevOps isn't  something you do it is something that  you believe as an organization which in  turn drives you to do things that drive  your core business if you believe  following DevOps tools following DevOps  best practices will give a boost to your  business will impact your return on  investment would affect your bottom line  then that is how you begin to do things  DevOps is a very different philosophy  than how we used to do projects earlier  in the past QA and operation were  usually separate teams from the  development team within the IT code  would go from a developer to QA and back  and forth till QA passed all the tests  and then operation worked on deploying  the code the intent of having these  gates between different roads was  basically to make sure that nobody did  anything that wasn't supposed to be done  like deploying an unapproved or untested  code into production while this was  implemented or put in place with all the  good intent the end result of this led  to developers becoming lazy knowing that  another team which is the QA team is now  going to check their work so they  concentrated less and less on producing  quality code because they knew even if  they missed something or if they poorly  coded something there is an entire team  dedicated to find any mistakes they did  even while doing unit testing many times  a developer would not even do unit  testing just push the code in the  testing knowing that there are hundreds  of test cases that are going to run so  invariably if they made any mistake it  will get caught  QA intern had to start taking their job  more seriously an organization started  to invest heavily in QA automation  because they not only had to do the  functional testing but many ways they  had to even find falls within the unit  testing or any poorly coded coding  projects that the developers had  followed since the developers were doing  their job less well the organization had  to spend a lot of time and money on  testing this also led to the developer  versus QA approach not being very  beneficial for end software quality  because if there are fundamental issues  with the code written how much ever you  can test you will not be able to find  out all the issues and resolve them in  the time that you have for your project  to be completed and also be able to  produce a quality deliverable  unfortunately the organization's  compensation and reward systems also  heavily supported this approach and this  also led to enter team rivalry making  for a very  unpleasant and unproductive workspace  operation in turn made mistakes because  they did not write the code developers  were laid back and had little motivation  to write code that was easy to deploy  manage or monitor developers basically  threw the code over the wall and then  operations just had to deal with it  this calls further inter team rivalry  between development and operations team  because everyone in the organization was  appraised on the project delivery and  how quickly they were able to deliver it  and how few issues there were so more  issues more problems by operations men  their appraisals were not good and  developers had little reason to start  writing better code because QA was being  questioned for not doing sufficient  testing before the project release went  for production deployment  software development and delivery teams  were all responsible to have the same  goal delivering high quality software  and services to your end customer so we  are all supposed to be in this together  all the teams your business your  development your testing your operations  your stakeholders it is not a matter of  throwing things over the wall and  dusting your hands off that my job is  done so now it is all up to you to know  how to take it into production  DevOps on the other hand envisions  application and software delivery that  can be constantly pushed in small  incremental updates to the end-users  with minimum of operational overhead  small releases are not only easier to  code and test but also far safer to push  into production on an ongoing basis but  in order for all of that to happen  everyone has to work together in a  DevOps environment this is how things  work developers write the code they  check their code into a source code  repository repositories current code is  pooled and built into a software  application all the automated tests  created by developers are run including  integration tests and even user  acceptance test if tests succeed then  the build gets deployed automatically  into production and your end user  feedback is collected which goes into  the next iteration of the development  cycle and you go on repeating these  steps so on and so forth  in a DevOps environment many parts of  this can be automated all these steps  that we just saw parts like user  acceptance testing can perhaps still be  done manually the objective here is to  create as few barriers as possible  between the developer and the end user  that certainly does not mean there are  no checks that is where testing comes in  however you don't put one part of the IT  team in charge of stopping another part  of the IT team from doing stupid things  you need the ability to automatically  and constantly spin up software  environments creating a variety of  authorized roles within your development  team to start environments on-demand it  could be a developer spinning up a  development test environment several  times during the day environments that  are spun up must reflect the final  production environment if you as an  organization cannot come to terms with  allowing your developers to automate  this process then DevOps is something  that you are still not ready for  Neuer configuration management tools  like your microsoft's desired state  configuration chef salt puppet and  ansible let you do all of this  containerization is another technology  that can work as it abstracts a number  of environment variables reducing  variations and complexity between  environments by doing this any  environment whether you are development  test or production the application runs  exactly the same all the time you are  much less likely to run into problems  moving the code from one environment to  another if by adopting all this  automation and tooling you are still  making one team a bottleneck to move  from one step of checking in your code  into source code repository to  triggering a build on that and that  really doesn't help you with the  automation because now you have an away  created manual hurdles or team hurdles  just to trigger these automated tasks  you need to provide developers with a  managed set of capabilities developers  working on a project should be able to  spin up a virtual environment without  anyone else's intervention they should  be able to recycle environments even  delete and recreate anytime they want  they do not get to change their  environment specifications on their own  or get free rein of yet to be created  virtualization infrastructure so these  could be some of the very fundamental  changes that you also need to implement  on the ground when adopting DevOps to  build an environment of your people of  your team's that can support DevOps  you need to have the confidence and  trust in your teams to allow them to do  all of these automated tasks if Trust is  an issue or the capabilities of your  team members is an issue then you have a  separate problem that needs to be solved  but without all this going hand in hand  with latest tools and technology you  will not be able to benefit from DevOps  only as much you will unfortunately not  be able to get the full benefit of  adopting DevOps  hey once become an expert in cloud  computing then subscribe to simpler  Channel and click here to watch more  such videos turn it up and get certified  in cloud computing click here

